{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Six days shall it be done.</b> The reason for the mention of the doing of melakhah on the weekdays is perhaps to say that if the seventh day will be holy, the melakhah you need shall be done during the six days." "<b>Or</b> the verse may intend to prohibit melakhah of Shabbat both through oneself and through a non-Jew, and this is its saying <b>six days shall it be done</b> — with a tzerei under the tav, meaning: it shall be done; but on the seventh day, even through others, you may not do it. This is as they say: instructing a non-Jew is prohibited by Torah law (Mekhilta, parashat Bo). And the reason it says <b>six</b> and not בששת is so that you not err and think there is a commandment to do melakhah on them." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes that the six days will not stand in their melakhah which HaShem prepared them to do, unless the seventh day is holy to you; but if they do not keep Shabbat the six days will not endure, for Shabbat is the soul of the world's existence — as we introduced in parashat Bereshit in the interpretation of the verse (Genesis 2:2) ״וַיְכַל וגו׳ בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי״ — see there."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {1 "The phrase בצירי״י תחת התי״ו refers to the vowel tzerei under the tav in תֵּעָשֶׂה (the nif'al form), as opposed to a hypothetical form with tav prefixed by another consonant; resolved the spelling per context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:49.181089044Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}